<Prose title="Support" description="Answers to common questions about LanDeck, and how to get help.">
  <p class="intro">Answers to the questions people ask most. Can’t find yours? <a href={site.supportUrl}>Ask on GitHub</a>.</p>

  <div class="faq">
    <details>
      <summary>LanDeck says Local Network access is off. How do I turn it on?</summary>
      <div>
        <p>
          Open System Settings › Privacy &amp; Security › Local Network and switch LanDeck on. LanDeck has a button that
          opens that page. Come back to LanDeck and it scans again by itself.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Why doesn’t it show my Wi‑Fi network’s name?</summary>
      <div>
        <p>
          macOS only shares the Wi‑Fi name with apps that have Location access. Allow it when LanDeck asks, or in System
          Settings › Privacy &amp; Security › Location Services. LanDeck never uses your location. Without it, the network
          goes by its address, such as 192.168.1.0/24, and you can give it any name you like.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>A device is missing from the list.</summary>
      <div>
        <ul>
          <li>It may be asleep. Phones and some smart-home devices drop off the network to save power; wake it and scan again.</li>
          <li>It may be on another network: a guest Wi‑Fi, or a second router. LanDeck sees the network your Mac is on.</li>
          <li>Some routers and mesh systems keep wireless devices from seeing each other (often called “client isolation” or “AP isolation”).</li>
          <li>On a large network, LanDeck first sweeps the 254 addresses around your Mac. Choose Scan All in the banner to sweep the whole network.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Why is a phone shown as a new device again?</summary>
      <div>
        <p>
          iPhones and Android phones use a private Wi‑Fi address that can change, which looks like a new device. LanDeck
          recognises the phone when its name is distinctive, such as “Sara’s iPhone”. You can also switch the phone’s
          Private Wi‑Fi Address to Fixed for your own network in its Wi‑Fi settings.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>How do I get alerts when a new device joins?</summary>
      <div>
        <p>
          That’s part of LanDeck Pro. Turn on Settings › Monitoring › Scan in the background, and allow notifications when
          macOS asks. LanDeck needs to be open, in its window or the menu bar; turn on Open LanDeck at login in Settings ›
          General to keep it running.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Wake-on-LAN doesn’t wake my computer.</summary>
      <div>
        <p>
          The computer has to allow it: on a Mac, it’s “Wake for network access” in System Settings › Energy (or Battery);
          on a PC, in the network adapter’s settings and often in the BIOS. Most computers can only be woken over Ethernet,
          not Wi‑Fi.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>What does a security finding mean, and what should I do?</summary>
      <div>
        <p>
          Each finding in Security explains the risk and what to do about it. Findings are grouped as Fix soon, Worth a look
          and Good to know. When you’ve dealt with one, the next scan no longer shows it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Security says the router’s hardware address changed.</summary>
      <div>
        <p>
          If you or your provider replaced the router, choose This Is My New Router, and LanDeck knows it from then on. If
          not, restart the router and check for devices you don’t know: another device pretending to be the router is how
          someone listens in on a network.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Where does LanDeck keep its data?</summary>
      <div>
        <p>On your Mac only:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Mac App Store: <code>~/Library/Containers/net.othi.landeck/Data/Library/Application Support/LanDeck/</code></li>
          <li>Direct download: <code>~/Library/Application Support/LanDeck/</code></li>
        </ul>
        <p><code>library.json</code> has your devices and changes; <code>history.sqlite</code> has the history (LanDeck Pro).</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>How do I cancel LanDeck Pro?</summary>
      <div>
        <p>
          In the App Store, click your name, then Account Settings › Subscriptions › Manage. Or open LanDeck › Settings ›
          LanDeck Pro › Manage Subscription. Pro stays on until the end of the period you paid for.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Is my data private?</summary>
      <div>
        <p>
          Yes. Nothing about your network leaves your Mac, and LanDeck has no analytics or account. The
          <a href={url('privacy/')}>privacy policy</a> has the details.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>
  </div>

  <h2>Still stuck?</h2>
  <p>
    Open an issue on <a href={site.supportUrl}>GitHub</a> with your macOS version, the LanDeck version (LanDeck › About
    LanDeck) and what you see. Please leave out addresses and names you’d rather keep private.
  </p>
</Prose>
